1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,962
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,142 --> 00:00:04,093
If you ask ten wealthy people how

3
00:00:04,093 --> 00:00:05,453
they built their money,

4
00:00:05,453 --> 00:00:07,819
you'll hear three answers over and over.

5
00:00:07,999 --> 00:00:08,637
Stocks.

6
00:00:08,817 --> 00:00:09,851
Real estate.

7
00:00:10,031 --> 00:00:10,994
Or a business.

8
00:00:11,174 --> 00:00:14,216
Each one has created millions of millionaires.

9
00:00:14,396 --> 00:00:16,544
Each one has also ruined people who

10
00:00:16,544 --> 00:00:17,833
didn't understand it.

11
00:00:18,013 --> 00:00:20,613
So which one builds wealth faster?

12
00:00:20,793 --> 00:00:23,312
Today we're putting all three side by

13
00:00:23,312 --> 00:00:24,946
side, with real numbers,

14
00:00:24,946 --> 00:00:27,669
real psychology and a practical plan for

15
00:00:27,669 --> 00:00:28,282
choosing.

16
00:00:28,632 --> 00:00:29,851
Here's the short answer.

17
00:00:30,031 --> 00:00:31,238
There isn't one winner.

18
00:00:31,418 --> 00:00:33,765
Stocks are the easiest and most hands

19
00:00:33,765 --> 00:00:34,019
off.

20
00:00:34,199 --> 00:00:37,087
Real estate adds leverage and steady rent,

21
00:00:37,087 --> 00:00:38,738
but takes work and cash.

22
00:00:38,918 --> 00:00:41,145
A business has the highest potential,

23
00:00:41,145 --> 00:00:43,191
and the highest chance of failure.

24
00:00:43,371 --> 00:00:45,326
The best choice depends on your money,

25
00:00:45,326 --> 00:00:47,179
your time and your stomach for risk.

26
00:00:47,529 --> 00:00:49,258
Think of them like three vehicles.

27
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:51,366
Stocks are a reliable train.

28
00:00:51,546 --> 00:00:52,699
You buy a ticket,

29
00:00:52,699 --> 00:00:54,734
sit down and let it carry you,

30
00:00:54,734 --> 00:00:56,090
slowly but steadily,

31
00:00:56,090 --> 00:00:57,989
with occasional scary bumps.

32
00:00:58,169 --> 00:01:00,131
Real estate is a truck.

33
00:01:00,311 --> 00:01:01,942
It can carry a heavy load,

34
00:01:01,942 --> 00:01:03,823
especially with borrowed fuel,

35
00:01:03,823 --> 00:01:05,956
but you have to drive and maintain

36
00:01:05,956 --> 00:01:06,708
it yourself.

37
00:01:06,888 --> 00:01:08,885
A business is a race car.

38
00:01:09,065 --> 00:01:10,708
It can go incredibly fast,

39
00:01:10,708 --> 00:01:12,035
but it crashes often,

40
00:01:12,035 --> 00:01:14,058
and the driver needs real skill.

41
00:01:14,408 --> 00:01:16,880
We'll compare them from three angles.

42
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:18,454
First, the numbers.

43
00:01:18,634 --> 00:01:20,178
Long term returns,

44
00:01:20,178 --> 00:01:22,237
leverage, failure rates,

45
00:01:22,237 --> 00:01:24,125
costs, taxes and time.

46
00:01:24,305 --> 00:01:26,116
Second, the psychology.

47
00:01:26,296 --> 00:01:28,472
Why people fall in love with one

48
00:01:28,472 --> 00:01:29,628
and fear another,

49
00:01:29,628 --> 00:01:31,532
often for the wrong reasons.

50
00:01:31,712 --> 00:01:33,268
And third, the playbook.

51
00:01:33,448 --> 00:01:35,574
How to choose and combine them based

52
00:01:35,574 --> 00:01:36,873
on your stage of life.

53
00:01:37,053 --> 00:01:39,242
Reminder, this is education,

54
00:01:39,242 --> 00:01:40,883
not financial advice,

55
00:01:40,883 --> 00:01:43,618
and all examples are illustrations,

56
00:01:43,618 --> 00:01:44,634
not promises.

57
00:01:44,985 --> 00:01:46,598
Let's start with the numbers.

58
00:01:46,778 --> 00:01:48,644
And first, the one most people can

59
00:01:48,644 --> 00:01:49,576
start with today.

60
00:01:49,756 --> 00:01:50,476
Stocks.

61
00:01:50,827 --> 00:01:51,662
Our numbers.

62
00:01:51,842 --> 00:01:55,280
Stock market history comes from sources like

63
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:57,311
S and P Dow Jones Indices.

64
00:01:57,491 --> 00:02:00,069
Long run comparisons of housing and stock

65
00:02:00,069 --> 00:02:02,585
returns come from the research paper The

66
00:02:02,585 --> 00:02:04,912
Rate of Return on Everything by Oscar

67
00:02:04,912 --> 00:02:06,233
Jorda and colleagues.

68
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:09,634
Home price history comes from Robert Shiller's

69
00:02:09,634 --> 00:02:11,734
work and the S and P CoreLogic

70
00:02:11,734 --> 00:02:13,205
Case Shiller indices.

71
00:02:13,385 --> 00:02:15,753
Business survival data comes from the U.

72
00:02:15,933 --> 00:02:16,328
S.

73
00:02:16,508 --> 00:02:18,528
Bureau of Labor Statistics.

74
00:02:18,708 --> 00:02:21,946
Growth examples use stated average returns,

75
00:02:21,946 --> 00:02:24,281
as illustrations, not promises.

76
00:02:24,631 --> 00:02:25,339
Angle one.

77
00:02:25,519 --> 00:02:26,250
The numbers.

78
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:28,619
Seven ways to compare stocks,

79
00:02:28,619 --> 00:02:30,505
real estate and business.

80
00:02:30,856 --> 00:02:32,005
Lesson number one.

81
00:02:32,185 --> 00:02:34,605
Stocks have been a powerful long run

82
00:02:34,605 --> 00:02:35,076
engine.

83
00:02:35,426 --> 00:02:37,394
When you buy a broad stock index

84
00:02:37,394 --> 00:02:39,240
fund, you own a small piece of

85
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:41,393
hundreds or thousands of companies.

86
00:02:41,573 --> 00:02:43,773
Those companies employ people,

87
00:02:43,773 --> 00:02:46,194
invent products and earn profits.

88
00:02:46,374 --> 00:02:47,533
Over long periods,

89
00:02:47,533 --> 00:02:50,495
those profits have pushed stock prices higher.

90
00:02:50,675 --> 00:02:53,336
Historically, the S and P five hundred

91
00:02:53,336 --> 00:02:56,066
has averaged roughly ten percent a year

92
00:02:56,066 --> 00:02:58,726
before inflation, including dividends,

93
00:02:58,726 --> 00:02:59,987
over many decades.

94
00:03:00,337 --> 00:03:01,997
Here's what that can look like.

95
00:03:02,177 --> 00:03:05,318
Fifty thousand dollars invested for twenty years

96
00:03:05,318 --> 00:03:08,001
at a seven percent average yearly return,

97
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:10,161
a more conservative illustration,

98
00:03:10,161 --> 00:03:13,171
grows to roughly two hundred thousand dollars.

99
00:03:13,351 --> 00:03:14,381
At ten percent,

100
00:03:14,381 --> 00:03:17,194
roughly three hundred sixty six thousand.

101
00:03:17,374 --> 00:03:19,038
And you don't have to fix a

102
00:03:19,038 --> 00:03:21,011
roof, manage a tenant or hire an

103
00:03:21,011 --> 00:03:21,566
employee.

104
00:03:21,916 --> 00:03:23,529
The catch is volatility.

105
00:03:23,709 --> 00:03:25,413
Stocks can fall thirty,

106
00:03:25,413 --> 00:03:27,784
forty or even fifty percent in a

107
00:03:27,784 --> 00:03:30,896
bad market, and recoveries can take years.

108
00:03:31,076 --> 00:03:33,458
The long term average hides some very

109
00:03:33,458 --> 00:03:35,325
painful short term stretches.

110
00:03:35,676 --> 00:03:38,032
Dividends are part of that return too.

111
00:03:38,212 --> 00:03:41,370
Many companies share profits with shareholders as

112
00:03:41,370 --> 00:03:44,528
dividends, and reinvesting them buys more shares.

113
00:03:44,708 --> 00:03:45,924
Over long periods,

114
00:03:45,924 --> 00:03:49,032
reinvested dividends have made up a meaningful

115
00:03:49,032 --> 00:03:51,802
share of the stock market's total return.

116
00:03:51,982 --> 00:03:55,069
So stock investors get two engines working

117
00:03:55,069 --> 00:03:57,714
at once, rising company values and a

118
00:03:57,714 --> 00:03:59,772
growing stream of dividends.

119
00:04:00,123 --> 00:04:03,152
Stocks shine on one measure above all,

120
00:04:03,152 --> 00:04:03,710
effort.

121
00:04:03,890 --> 00:04:06,373
With low cost index funds and automatic

122
00:04:06,373 --> 00:04:09,046
investing, they can build wealth while you

123
00:04:09,046 --> 00:04:11,274
focus on your career and your life.

124
00:04:11,454 --> 00:04:13,484
No other path on this list asks

125
00:04:13,484 --> 00:04:14,925
for less of your time.

126
00:04:15,276 --> 00:04:16,390
Lesson number two.

127
00:04:16,570 --> 00:04:19,113
Real estate returns come from rent and

128
00:04:19,113 --> 00:04:19,983
appreciation.

129
00:04:20,334 --> 00:04:23,002
Many people think of real estate returns

130
00:04:23,002 --> 00:04:24,803
as just rising home prices.

131
00:04:24,983 --> 00:04:27,724
But Robert Shiller's research found that,

132
00:04:27,724 --> 00:04:29,262
adjusted for inflation,

133
00:04:29,262 --> 00:04:29,395
U.

134
00:04:29,575 --> 00:04:29,912
S.

135
00:04:30,092 --> 00:04:32,635
home prices rose only modestly over much

136
00:04:32,635 --> 00:04:34,225
of the twentieth century.

137
00:04:34,405 --> 00:04:37,055
Price growth alone hasn't usually been the

138
00:04:37,055 --> 00:04:37,749
main story.

139
00:04:38,099 --> 00:04:40,467
The bigger story for investors is rent.

140
00:04:40,647 --> 00:04:43,631
A rental property can produce income every

141
00:04:43,631 --> 00:04:45,976
month on top of any appreciation.

142
00:04:46,156 --> 00:04:48,722
A major study called The Rate of

143
00:04:48,722 --> 00:04:50,406
Return on Everything,

144
00:04:50,406 --> 00:04:54,015
covering sixteen countries from 1870 to 2015,

145
00:04:54,015 --> 00:04:56,742
found that housing's total return,

146
00:04:56,742 --> 00:04:58,586
rent plus price growth,

147
00:04:58,586 --> 00:05:01,874
was roughly comparable to stocks over the

148
00:05:01,874 --> 00:05:04,360
long run, with less volatility.

149
00:05:04,711 --> 00:05:07,605
But those research returns are averages,

150
00:05:07,605 --> 00:05:09,703
before many real world costs.

151
00:05:09,883 --> 00:05:11,628
Property taxes, insurance,

152
00:05:11,628 --> 00:05:15,117
repairs, vacancies, property management and the time

153
00:05:15,117 --> 00:05:17,734
you spend all reduce what an individual

154
00:05:17,734 --> 00:05:19,345
landlord actually earns.

155
00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:21,471
There's also the question of what you'd

156
00:05:21,471 --> 00:05:22,702
pay to live there yourself.

157
00:05:22,882 --> 00:05:24,666
A home you live in saves you

158
00:05:24,666 --> 00:05:27,024
rent, which is a real economic return

159
00:05:27,024 --> 00:05:28,872
even though no check arrives.

160
00:05:29,052 --> 00:05:31,734
Economists call this imputed rent.

161
00:05:31,914 --> 00:05:34,045
It's one reason owning a home can

162
00:05:34,045 --> 00:05:36,564
make financial sense over long periods,

163
00:05:36,564 --> 00:05:39,147
even when the price itself grows slowly.

164
00:05:39,498 --> 00:05:41,703
Real estate is also local.

165
00:05:41,883 --> 00:05:45,018
One neighborhood can boom while another declines.

166
00:05:45,198 --> 00:05:46,671
Unlike an index fund,

167
00:05:46,671 --> 00:05:49,686
a single property means your result depends

168
00:05:49,686 --> 00:05:51,369
heavily on one location,

169
00:05:51,369 --> 00:05:53,894
one building and one set of tenants.

170
00:05:54,244 --> 00:05:55,439
Lesson number three.

171
00:05:55,619 --> 00:05:57,890
Leverage magnifies results,

172
00:05:57,890 --> 00:05:58,731
both ways.

173
00:05:59,081 --> 00:06:02,064
Real estate's secret weapon is leverage.

174
00:06:02,244 --> 00:06:04,520
Most people buy property with a mortgage.

175
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:06,598
Say you buy a three hundred thousand

176
00:06:06,598 --> 00:06:09,181
dollar property with sixty thousand dollars down.

177
00:06:09,361 --> 00:06:11,752
If its value rises four percent in

178
00:06:11,752 --> 00:06:14,495
a year, that's twelve thousand dollars,

179
00:06:14,495 --> 00:06:16,816
which is a twenty percent gain on

180
00:06:16,816 --> 00:06:18,223
your sixty thousand,

181
00:06:18,223 --> 00:06:19,137
before costs.

182
00:06:19,487 --> 00:06:21,739
But leverage works in reverse too.

183
00:06:21,919 --> 00:06:24,105
If the property falls ten percent,

184
00:06:24,105 --> 00:06:26,228
you lose thirty thousand dollars,

185
00:06:26,228 --> 00:06:27,900
half of your down payment,

186
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:28,479
on paper.

187
00:06:28,659 --> 00:06:31,039
During the housing crash around 2008,

188
00:06:31,039 --> 00:06:33,355
many owners ended up owing more than

189
00:06:33,355 --> 00:06:34,835
their homes were worth.

190
00:06:35,185 --> 00:06:37,659
You can also use leverage with stocks,

191
00:06:37,659 --> 00:06:39,026
through margin loans,

192
00:06:39,026 --> 00:06:41,825
but most experts strongly discourage it for

193
00:06:41,825 --> 00:06:45,015
regular investors, because margin calls can force

194
00:06:45,015 --> 00:06:46,969
you to sell at the worst time.

195
00:06:47,149 --> 00:06:49,666
Mortgages are generally more forgiving,

196
00:06:49,666 --> 00:06:52,119
with fixed payments and no daily price

197
00:06:52,119 --> 00:06:52,571
checks.

198
00:06:52,921 --> 00:06:55,681
Leverage also changes how you should measure

199
00:06:55,681 --> 00:06:56,183
returns.

200
00:06:56,363 --> 00:06:59,220
Real estate investors often talk about cash

201
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:00,217
on cash return,

202
00:07:00,217 --> 00:07:02,675
the yearly cash profit divided by the

203
00:07:02,675 --> 00:07:03,805
cash they put in.

204
00:07:03,985 --> 00:07:07,009
A property earning three thousand dollars a

205
00:07:07,009 --> 00:07:09,681
year after all costs on sixty thousand

206
00:07:09,681 --> 00:07:12,212
dollars down has a five percent cash

207
00:07:12,212 --> 00:07:13,267
on cash return,

208
00:07:13,267 --> 00:07:16,361
before any appreciation or mortgage paydown.

209
00:07:16,712 --> 00:07:18,889
The key to safe leverage is cash

210
00:07:18,889 --> 00:07:20,113
flow and reserves.

211
00:07:20,293 --> 00:07:22,254
If the rent covers the mortgage and

212
00:07:22,254 --> 00:07:24,551
costs, and you have cash for emergencies,

213
00:07:24,551 --> 00:07:26,063
you can survive a downturn.

214
00:07:26,243 --> 00:07:28,701
If not, leverage can turn a bad

215
00:07:28,701 --> 00:07:30,365
year into a disaster.

216
00:07:30,715 --> 00:07:31,899
Lesson number four.

217
00:07:32,079 --> 00:07:34,306
A business has the highest ceiling,

218
00:07:34,306 --> 00:07:35,643
and the highest risk.

219
00:07:35,993 --> 00:07:38,067
Many of the wealthiest people in the

220
00:07:38,067 --> 00:07:40,834
world built their fortunes by owning businesses.

221
00:07:41,014 --> 00:07:43,479
Federal Reserve survey data has shown that

222
00:07:43,479 --> 00:07:45,944
privately owned businesses make up a large

223
00:07:45,944 --> 00:07:48,468
share of wealth for the richest households.

224
00:07:48,648 --> 00:07:50,748
No other path can turn a small

225
00:07:50,748 --> 00:07:52,778
amount of money into so much,

226
00:07:52,778 --> 00:07:53,338
so fast.

227
00:07:53,688 --> 00:07:55,499
But the failure rate is high.

228
00:07:55,679 --> 00:07:58,381
According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics,

229
00:07:58,381 --> 00:08:00,776
roughly one in five new businesses fail

230
00:08:00,776 --> 00:08:02,004
in their first year,

231
00:08:02,004 --> 00:08:03,969
and around half don't make it to

232
00:08:03,969 --> 00:08:04,584
year five.

233
00:08:04,764 --> 00:08:06,983
Many owners lose their savings and years

234
00:08:06,983 --> 00:08:07,538
of effort.

235
00:08:07,889 --> 00:08:11,412
A successful business also creates value in

236
00:08:11,412 --> 00:08:12,149
two ways.

237
00:08:12,329 --> 00:08:13,618
It produces income,

238
00:08:13,618 --> 00:08:14,907
and it can be sold.

239
00:08:15,087 --> 00:08:17,554
Small businesses often sell for a few

240
00:08:17,554 --> 00:08:19,287
times their yearly profit,

241
00:08:19,287 --> 00:08:22,288
so building a profitable business also builds

242
00:08:22,288 --> 00:08:24,688
an asset you can eventually cash in.

243
00:08:25,039 --> 00:08:27,396
Business returns also depend on what you

244
00:08:27,396 --> 00:08:28,162
pay yourself.

245
00:08:28,342 --> 00:08:31,569
Many small business owners earn something closer

246
00:08:31,569 --> 00:08:33,384
to a salary for long hours,

247
00:08:33,384 --> 00:08:35,401
especially in the early years.

248
00:08:35,581 --> 00:08:37,705
The real wealth often comes later,

249
00:08:37,705 --> 00:08:40,016
when the business can run without the

250
00:08:40,016 --> 00:08:41,016
owner every day,

251
00:08:41,016 --> 00:08:42,140
or when it's sold.

252
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:45,148
A business that depends completely on you

253
00:08:45,148 --> 00:08:47,423
is more like a demanding job than

254
00:08:47,423 --> 00:08:48,044
an asset.

255
00:08:48,394 --> 00:08:50,854
The business path rewards skill,

256
00:08:50,854 --> 00:08:52,469
effort and some luck.

257
00:08:52,649 --> 00:08:54,801
It's less of an investment and more

258
00:08:54,801 --> 00:08:55,969
of a second career.

259
00:08:56,149 --> 00:08:57,395
For the right person,

260
00:08:57,395 --> 00:08:58,819
it can be life changing.

261
00:08:58,999 --> 00:09:00,097
For the wrong one,

262
00:09:00,097 --> 00:09:01,438
it can be devastating.

263
00:09:01,788 --> 00:09:03,146
Lesson number five.

264
00:09:03,326 --> 00:09:05,845
Costs and friction differ enormously.

265
00:09:06,196 --> 00:09:08,572
Buying a broad index fund can cost

266
00:09:08,572 --> 00:09:09,621
almost nothing.

267
00:09:09,801 --> 00:09:12,153
Many brokerages charge no commissions,

268
00:09:12,153 --> 00:09:14,135
and the best funds charge only a

269
00:09:14,135 --> 00:09:16,302
few hundredths of a percent a year.

270
00:09:16,482 --> 00:09:18,885
You can buy or sell in seconds.

271
00:09:19,236 --> 00:09:22,334
Real estate is expensive to buy and

272
00:09:22,334 --> 00:09:22,777
sell.

273
00:09:22,957 --> 00:09:24,832
Closing costs, inspections,

274
00:09:24,832 --> 00:09:27,958
loan fees, agent commissions and moving costs

275
00:09:27,958 --> 00:09:30,597
can add up to a significant percentage

276
00:09:30,597 --> 00:09:33,376
of the property's price when you include

277
00:09:33,376 --> 00:09:35,043
both buying and selling.

278
00:09:35,223 --> 00:09:37,749
That's why short holding periods often lose

279
00:09:37,749 --> 00:09:38,102
money.

280
00:09:38,452 --> 00:09:40,959
Businesses can be the most expensive of

281
00:09:40,959 --> 00:09:41,795
all to start.

282
00:09:41,975 --> 00:09:44,426
Equipment, inventory, rent,

283
00:09:44,426 --> 00:09:48,057
licenses, marketing and months of low or

284
00:09:48,057 --> 00:09:48,964
no income.

285
00:09:49,144 --> 00:09:51,977
Many businesses take years to become profitable.

286
00:09:52,328 --> 00:09:54,232
There are ongoing costs too.

287
00:09:54,412 --> 00:09:57,409
Index funds charge a small yearly expense

288
00:09:57,409 --> 00:09:57,848
ratio.

289
00:09:58,028 --> 00:10:00,375
Properties need regular maintenance,

290
00:10:00,375 --> 00:10:03,113
often estimated by landlords at around one

291
00:10:03,113 --> 00:10:05,786
percent of the property's value per year,

292
00:10:05,786 --> 00:10:07,351
though it varies widely.

293
00:10:07,531 --> 00:10:09,761
Businesses face rising rent,

294
00:10:09,761 --> 00:10:12,628
wages, software and insurance costs.

295
00:10:12,808 --> 00:10:14,542
Always count ongoing costs,

296
00:10:14,542 --> 00:10:16,662
not just the price of getting in.

297
00:10:17,013 --> 00:10:19,602
The lesson is that friction rewards patience.

298
00:10:19,782 --> 00:10:22,019
The more expensive an investment is to

299
00:10:22,019 --> 00:10:22,784
buy and sell,

300
00:10:22,784 --> 00:10:24,550
the longer you need to hold it

301
00:10:24,550 --> 00:10:25,610
to come out ahead.

302
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:27,341
Lesson number six.

303
00:10:27,521 --> 00:10:30,215
Each path has different tax advantages.

304
00:10:30,565 --> 00:10:33,526
Stocks can be held in tax advantaged

305
00:10:33,526 --> 00:10:35,994
accounts like a four oh one k,

306
00:10:35,994 --> 00:10:37,310
IRA or Roth IRA.

307
00:10:37,490 --> 00:10:38,819
In regular accounts,

308
00:10:38,819 --> 00:10:41,077
gains aren't taxed until you sell,

309
00:10:41,077 --> 00:10:43,535
and long term gains usually get lower

310
00:10:43,535 --> 00:10:43,934
rates.

311
00:10:44,114 --> 00:10:45,415
That's a powerful,

312
00:10:45,415 --> 00:10:46,644
simple tax setup.

313
00:10:46,995 --> 00:10:50,236
Real estate investors can deduct expenses and

314
00:10:50,236 --> 00:10:54,053
depreciation, which can reduce taxable rental income.

315
00:10:54,233 --> 00:10:57,080
Some can defer gains by exchanging one

316
00:10:57,080 --> 00:11:00,525
investment property for another through a 1031

317
00:11:00,525 --> 00:11:01,199
exchange.

318
00:11:01,379 --> 00:11:03,856
And homeowners can often exclude a large

319
00:11:03,856 --> 00:11:06,147
amount of gain when selling a primary

320
00:11:06,147 --> 00:11:08,253
residence, if they meet the rules.

321
00:11:08,603 --> 00:11:12,232
Business owners can deduct ordinary and necessary

322
00:11:12,232 --> 00:11:16,232
business expenses, may qualify for special deductions,

323
00:11:16,232 --> 00:11:18,898
and can open retirement plans like a

324
00:11:18,898 --> 00:11:20,305
solo four oh one k.

325
00:11:20,485 --> 00:11:23,061
But business taxes are complex and often

326
00:11:23,061 --> 00:11:24,735
require professional help.

327
00:11:25,085 --> 00:11:28,092
The simplest tax advantage of all may

328
00:11:28,092 --> 00:11:29,392
be the Roth IRA.

329
00:11:29,572 --> 00:11:33,360
Qualified withdrawals, including decades of growth,

330
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:35,365
can be completely tax free.

331
00:11:35,545 --> 00:11:36,660
For most people,

332
00:11:36,660 --> 00:11:40,282
maximizing tax advantaged retirement accounts is the

333
00:11:40,282 --> 00:11:42,163
first and easiest tax move,

334
00:11:42,163 --> 00:11:44,531
no matter which of the three paths

335
00:11:44,531 --> 00:11:45,994
they eventually take.

336
00:11:46,345 --> 00:11:48,861
Tax rules change often and have many

337
00:11:48,861 --> 00:11:49,630
conditions.

338
00:11:49,810 --> 00:11:52,274
Check current IRS guidance or a tax

339
00:11:52,274 --> 00:11:55,371
professional before relying on any of these.

340
00:11:55,722 --> 00:11:57,103
Lesson number seven.

341
00:11:57,283 --> 00:11:58,148
Count your time,

342
00:11:58,148 --> 00:11:59,715
and how fast you can get your

343
00:11:59,715 --> 00:12:00,255
money out.

344
00:12:00,606 --> 00:12:02,533
Returns don't tell the whole story.

345
00:12:02,713 --> 00:12:05,089
If a rental earns two thousand dollars

346
00:12:05,089 --> 00:12:07,028
more a year than an index fund,

347
00:12:07,028 --> 00:12:09,217
but takes two hundred hours of your

348
00:12:09,217 --> 00:12:12,156
time, you're effectively earning ten dollars an

349
00:12:12,156 --> 00:12:13,719
hour for that extra work.

350
00:12:13,899 --> 00:12:15,762
It's worth asking what your time is

351
00:12:15,762 --> 00:12:16,082
worth.

352
00:12:16,432 --> 00:12:17,860
Liquidity matters too.

353
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:20,244
You can sell index fund shares on

354
00:12:20,244 --> 00:12:21,848
almost any business day.

355
00:12:22,028 --> 00:12:24,152
Selling a property can take months.

356
00:12:24,332 --> 00:12:26,568
Selling a business can take a year

357
00:12:26,568 --> 00:12:28,803
or more, and sometimes it can't be

358
00:12:28,803 --> 00:12:29,592
sold at all.

359
00:12:29,942 --> 00:12:32,229
Stocks are high liquidity and low time.

360
00:12:32,409 --> 00:12:35,451
Real estate is lower liquidity and medium

361
00:12:35,451 --> 00:12:35,822
time.

362
00:12:36,002 --> 00:12:38,853
A business is usually the lowest liquidity

363
00:12:38,853 --> 00:12:40,008
and highest time.

364
00:12:40,188 --> 00:12:41,453
That doesn't make one better.

365
00:12:41,633 --> 00:12:43,467
It makes them suited to different people.

366
00:12:43,818 --> 00:12:45,617
Time can also be bought.

367
00:12:45,797 --> 00:12:48,148
Landlords can hire property managers,

368
00:12:48,148 --> 00:12:50,371
often for a percentage of the rent.

369
00:12:50,551 --> 00:12:53,918
Business owners can hire employees and managers.

370
00:12:54,098 --> 00:12:55,209
That frees time,

371
00:12:55,209 --> 00:12:57,152
but it also reduces returns.

372
00:12:57,332 --> 00:12:59,766
The question is always whether the extra

373
00:12:59,766 --> 00:13:01,653
return is worth the extra time,

374
00:13:01,653 --> 00:13:03,722
or whether you'd rather pay to get

375
00:13:03,722 --> 00:13:04,634
your time back.

376
00:13:04,985 --> 00:13:05,855
Try this.

377
00:13:06,035 --> 00:13:07,909
Estimate how many hours a month you

378
00:13:07,909 --> 00:13:10,639
could realistically give to building wealth outside

379
00:13:10,639 --> 00:13:11,120
your job.

380
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:13,051
Your answer is a big clue about

381
00:13:13,051 --> 00:13:14,180
which path fits you.

382
00:13:14,530 --> 00:13:15,447
That's angle one.

383
00:13:15,627 --> 00:13:18,002
Stocks as the long run engine,

384
00:13:18,002 --> 00:13:20,456
real estate's rent plus growth,

385
00:13:20,456 --> 00:13:23,228
leverage, business risk and reward,

386
00:13:23,228 --> 00:13:26,157
costs, taxes, and time and liquidity.

387
00:13:26,337 --> 00:13:27,614
Now angle two.

388
00:13:27,794 --> 00:13:28,804
The psychology.

389
00:13:29,155 --> 00:13:31,161
People rarely choose stocks,

390
00:13:31,161 --> 00:13:33,597
real estate or a business based on

391
00:13:33,597 --> 00:13:34,600
a spreadsheet.

392
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:36,762
They choose based on feelings,

393
00:13:36,762 --> 00:13:39,075
family stories and what feels safe.

394
00:13:39,255 --> 00:13:42,318
Here are seven psychological forces that shape

395
00:13:42,318 --> 00:13:45,114
these choices, sometimes in ways that cost

396
00:13:45,114 --> 00:13:45,513
money.

397
00:13:45,863 --> 00:13:47,024
Lesson number eight.

398
00:13:47,204 --> 00:13:48,794
Things you can touch feel safer.

399
00:13:49,144 --> 00:13:51,421
Many people trust real estate more than

400
00:13:51,421 --> 00:13:53,232
stocks because they can see it,

401
00:13:53,232 --> 00:13:54,925
walk through it and touch it.

402
00:13:55,105 --> 00:13:56,789
A house feels real.

403
00:13:56,969 --> 00:13:58,951
A stock feels like a number on

404
00:13:58,951 --> 00:13:59,546
a screen.

405
00:13:59,726 --> 00:14:02,606
That tangibility gives real estate a sense

406
00:14:02,606 --> 00:14:04,115
of safety and control.

407
00:14:04,465 --> 00:14:07,216
But tangible doesn't automatically mean safer.

408
00:14:07,396 --> 00:14:10,318
A single property is concentrated in one

409
00:14:10,318 --> 00:14:13,387
location, and can face problems like major

410
00:14:13,387 --> 00:14:16,382
repairs, bad tenants or a local downturn.

411
00:14:16,562 --> 00:14:19,091
An index fund is spread across hundreds

412
00:14:19,091 --> 00:14:19,999
of businesses.

413
00:14:20,349 --> 00:14:22,171
Stocks are also real.

414
00:14:22,351 --> 00:14:26,011
Each share represents ownership in actual companies,

415
00:14:26,011 --> 00:14:27,770
with buildings, products,

416
00:14:27,770 --> 00:14:29,178
workers and profits.

417
00:14:29,358 --> 00:14:31,115
It just doesn't feel that way because

418
00:14:31,115 --> 00:14:32,017
you don't see them.

419
00:14:32,367 --> 00:14:35,264
Real estate's sense of control is real

420
00:14:35,264 --> 00:14:36,256
in some ways.

421
00:14:36,436 --> 00:14:37,577
You can renovate,

422
00:14:37,577 --> 00:14:40,596
raise rents or improve management to increase

423
00:14:40,596 --> 00:14:40,999
value.

424
00:14:41,179 --> 00:14:42,620
With a stock index fund,

425
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:45,381
you can't change anything about the companies.

426
00:14:45,561 --> 00:14:47,349
Some people love that control.

427
00:14:47,529 --> 00:14:49,190
Others find it exhausting.

428
00:14:49,370 --> 00:14:51,144
Knowing which kind of person you are

429
00:14:51,144 --> 00:14:51,982
helps you choose.

430
00:14:52,332 --> 00:14:53,133
Try this.

431
00:14:53,313 --> 00:14:55,272
Look up the top ten holdings of

432
00:14:55,272 --> 00:14:56,157
an index fund.

433
00:14:56,337 --> 00:14:58,447
Seeing the real companies you own can

434
00:14:58,447 --> 00:15:00,329
make stocks feel as concrete as a

435
00:15:00,329 --> 00:15:00,842
building.

436
00:15:01,192 --> 00:15:02,445
Lesson number nine.

437
00:15:02,625 --> 00:15:05,226
Real estate's volatility is hidden.

438
00:15:05,576 --> 00:15:07,728
Stock prices update every second,

439
00:15:07,728 --> 00:15:09,163
so you see every drop.

440
00:15:09,343 --> 00:15:11,665
Home values aren't priced every day.

441
00:15:11,845 --> 00:15:13,934
You might only learn your home's value

442
00:15:13,934 --> 00:15:15,804
when you sell or get an appraisal.

443
00:15:15,984 --> 00:15:18,060
So real estate feels calmer,

444
00:15:18,060 --> 00:15:20,210
even when values are falling.

445
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:23,203
During the housing crash around 2008,

446
00:15:23,203 --> 00:15:25,632
national home prices fell sharply,

447
00:15:25,632 --> 00:15:27,560
and in some areas far more.

448
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:29,676
Many owners didn't feel it day to

449
00:15:29,676 --> 00:15:30,907
day, but it was real,

450
00:15:30,907 --> 00:15:32,667
and those who had to sell felt

451
00:15:32,667 --> 00:15:33,429
it painfully.

452
00:15:33,780 --> 00:15:37,245
Interestingly, that hidden volatility can help real

453
00:15:37,245 --> 00:15:38,400
estate investors.

454
00:15:38,580 --> 00:15:40,594
Because they don't see daily prices,

455
00:15:40,594 --> 00:15:42,551
they're less tempted to panic sell.

456
00:15:42,731 --> 00:15:44,923
Stock investors could learn from this by

457
00:15:44,923 --> 00:15:46,841
checking their accounts less often.

458
00:15:47,191 --> 00:15:50,012
Private businesses have hidden volatility too.

459
00:15:50,192 --> 00:15:52,192
Because there's no daily price,

460
00:15:52,192 --> 00:15:54,837
owners often feel their business is worth

461
00:15:54,837 --> 00:15:55,869
a steady amount.

462
00:15:56,049 --> 00:15:58,101
But the value of a small business

463
00:15:58,101 --> 00:16:00,153
can swing a lot with the economy,

464
00:16:00,153 --> 00:16:02,330
competition and the owner's health.

465
00:16:02,510 --> 00:16:04,578
The lack of a price tag doesn't

466
00:16:04,578 --> 00:16:05,912
mean a lack of risk.

467
00:16:06,262 --> 00:16:07,121
Try this.

468
00:16:07,301 --> 00:16:09,813
If daily stock prices make you anxious,

469
00:16:09,813 --> 00:16:12,583
check your investments only once a quarter.

470
00:16:12,763 --> 00:16:14,427
Treat them more like a house you

471
00:16:14,427 --> 00:16:15,259
own for decades.

472
00:16:15,610 --> 00:16:16,817
Lesson number ten.

473
00:16:16,997 --> 00:16:18,599
Survivorship bias.

474
00:16:18,950 --> 00:16:21,147
We hear about the founders who became

475
00:16:21,147 --> 00:16:24,058
billionaires and the landlords who built empires.

476
00:16:24,238 --> 00:16:26,550
We rarely hear about the businesses that

477
00:16:26,550 --> 00:16:29,036
closed or the investors who lost properties

478
00:16:29,036 --> 00:16:29,904
to foreclosure.

479
00:16:30,084 --> 00:16:32,139
That makes risky paths look safer than

480
00:16:32,139 --> 00:16:32,626
they are.

481
00:16:32,977 --> 00:16:36,591
Survivorship bias makes business ownership look like

482
00:16:36,591 --> 00:16:38,120
a sure path to riches.

483
00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:40,185
But for every famous founder,

484
00:16:40,185 --> 00:16:42,526
there are many who tried and failed,

485
00:16:42,526 --> 00:16:44,801
often losing savings along the way.

486
00:16:45,152 --> 00:16:47,624
It also affects real estate.

487
00:16:47,804 --> 00:16:50,544
Social media is full of investors showing

488
00:16:50,544 --> 00:16:53,552
their portfolios, but rarely their vacancies,

489
00:16:53,552 --> 00:16:55,757
repair bills or sleepless nights.

490
00:16:56,108 --> 00:16:58,697
Stocks have survivorship bias too.

491
00:16:58,877 --> 00:17:02,437
Market indexes regularly remove failing companies and

492
00:17:02,437 --> 00:17:03,579
add growing ones.

493
00:17:03,759 --> 00:17:05,961
That's why owning the whole index works

494
00:17:05,961 --> 00:17:08,163
better than owning a few companies that

495
00:17:08,163 --> 00:17:09,122
look great today.

496
00:17:09,302 --> 00:17:11,492
The index automatically keeps up with the

497
00:17:11,492 --> 00:17:13,468
survivors, so you don't have to guess

498
00:17:13,468 --> 00:17:14,643
which ones they'll be.

499
00:17:14,993 --> 00:17:15,840
Try this.

500
00:17:16,020 --> 00:17:17,910
When you hear a success story,

501
00:17:17,910 --> 00:17:20,052
ask how many people tried the same

502
00:17:20,052 --> 00:17:21,628
thing and didn't succeed.

503
00:17:21,808 --> 00:17:23,964
The answer changes how you see the

504
00:17:23,964 --> 00:17:24,281
odds.

505
00:17:24,631 --> 00:17:25,687
Quick pause.

506
00:17:25,867 --> 00:17:28,303
If this comparison is helping you think

507
00:17:28,303 --> 00:17:30,115
more clearly about your path,

508
00:17:30,115 --> 00:17:32,427
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

509
00:17:32,607 --> 00:17:34,414
It's free, and it helps us keep

510
00:17:34,414 --> 00:17:35,172
making these.

511
00:17:35,352 --> 00:17:37,071
Now, lesson eleven.

512
00:17:37,421 --> 00:17:38,732
Lesson number eleven.

513
00:17:38,912 --> 00:17:40,619
Choose what you can stick with.

514
00:17:40,970 --> 00:17:43,237
The best investment is often the one

515
00:17:43,237 --> 00:17:45,126
you'll stick with for decades.

516
00:17:45,306 --> 00:17:47,429
If you hate dealing with repairs and

517
00:17:47,429 --> 00:17:50,142
tenants, real estate might make you miserable,

518
00:17:50,142 --> 00:17:51,970
even if it earns more on paper.

519
00:17:52,150 --> 00:17:53,141
If you hate risk,

520
00:17:53,141 --> 00:17:54,947
a business could keep you up at

521
00:17:54,947 --> 00:17:55,296
night.

522
00:17:55,647 --> 00:17:57,926
Some people love the hands on work

523
00:17:57,926 --> 00:17:58,932
of real estate.

524
00:17:59,112 --> 00:18:01,251
Finding deals, renovating,

525
00:18:01,251 --> 00:18:02,897
managing properties.

526
00:18:03,077 --> 00:18:04,784
For them, it's not a chore.

527
00:18:04,964 --> 00:18:07,740
It's a satisfying project that also builds

528
00:18:07,740 --> 00:18:08,203
wealth.

529
00:18:08,553 --> 00:18:11,330
Others love building something from nothing,

530
00:18:11,330 --> 00:18:13,603
solving problems and leading people.

531
00:18:13,783 --> 00:18:16,291
For them, business ownership can be deeply

532
00:18:16,291 --> 00:18:17,962
rewarding, beyond the money.

533
00:18:18,313 --> 00:18:20,278
Your skills matter as much as your

534
00:18:20,278 --> 00:18:20,971
preferences.

535
00:18:21,151 --> 00:18:23,837
Someone who's handy with repairs has a

536
00:18:23,837 --> 00:18:25,958
real advantage in real estate.

537
00:18:26,138 --> 00:18:28,993
Someone with sales skills or deep industry

538
00:18:28,993 --> 00:18:31,304
knowledge has an edge in business.

539
00:18:31,484 --> 00:18:33,905
Someone with a busy career and no

540
00:18:33,905 --> 00:18:36,620
extra skills may get the best results

541
00:18:36,620 --> 00:18:38,381
from simple index funds.

542
00:18:38,561 --> 00:18:39,838
Use your strengths.

543
00:18:40,188 --> 00:18:40,954
Try this.

544
00:18:41,134 --> 00:18:43,068
Ask yourself which of these you'd do

545
00:18:43,068 --> 00:18:44,895
even if the returns were the same.

546
00:18:45,075 --> 00:18:47,041
That's often the path you'll stick with

547
00:18:47,041 --> 00:18:47,444
longest.

548
00:18:47,794 --> 00:18:49,024
Lesson number twelve.

549
00:18:49,204 --> 00:18:50,922
Pass the sleep test.

550
00:18:51,273 --> 00:18:53,664
A useful rule is the sleep test.

551
00:18:53,844 --> 00:18:55,923
If an investment keeps you awake at

552
00:18:55,923 --> 00:18:58,061
night, you probably have too much of

553
00:18:58,061 --> 00:18:59,783
it, or it's the wrong one for

554
00:18:59,783 --> 00:19:00,021
you.

555
00:19:00,201 --> 00:19:02,114
Stress leads to bad decisions,

556
00:19:02,114 --> 00:19:04,346
like panic selling or giving up too

557
00:19:04,346 --> 00:19:04,729
early.

558
00:19:05,079 --> 00:19:06,193
For some people,

559
00:19:06,193 --> 00:19:08,561
a stock market drop is terrifying,

560
00:19:08,561 --> 00:19:11,000
while a rental property feels calm.

561
00:19:11,180 --> 00:19:13,357
For others, the thought of a tenant

562
00:19:13,357 --> 00:19:15,909
calling about a broken heater at midnight

563
00:19:15,909 --> 00:19:16,718
is far worse.

564
00:19:16,898 --> 00:19:18,221
Risk isn't just math.

565
00:19:18,401 --> 00:19:19,237
It's personal.

566
00:19:19,586 --> 00:19:22,096
Business risk can be the most stressful

567
00:19:22,096 --> 00:19:23,898
of all, because your income,

568
00:19:23,898 --> 00:19:26,214
savings and identity can all be tied

569
00:19:26,214 --> 00:19:27,179
to one venture.

570
00:19:27,529 --> 00:19:30,339
Your risk tolerance also changes over time.

571
00:19:30,519 --> 00:19:32,841
Young people with stable jobs can often

572
00:19:32,841 --> 00:19:34,389
handle more ups and downs,

573
00:19:34,389 --> 00:19:36,591
because they have decades to recover.

574
00:19:36,771 --> 00:19:39,316
People close to retirement usually need more

575
00:19:39,316 --> 00:19:39,894
stability.

576
00:19:40,074 --> 00:19:42,031
A path that fits you at thirty

577
00:19:42,031 --> 00:19:43,662
may not fit you at sixty.

578
00:19:44,011 --> 00:19:44,871
Try this.

579
00:19:45,051 --> 00:19:47,633
Imagine your investment drops by a third

580
00:19:47,633 --> 00:19:48,278
next year.

581
00:19:48,458 --> 00:19:49,461
Would you hold,

582
00:19:49,461 --> 00:19:50,397
sell or panic?

583
00:19:50,577 --> 00:19:52,673
Your honest answer tells you how much

584
00:19:52,673 --> 00:19:53,410
risk to take.

585
00:19:53,759 --> 00:19:55,315
Lesson number thirteen.

586
00:19:55,495 --> 00:19:57,602
Concentration builds fortunes,

587
00:19:57,602 --> 00:19:59,710
diversification protects them.

588
00:20:00,059 --> 00:20:02,902
Many great fortunes come from concentration,

589
00:20:02,902 --> 00:20:05,293
putting most of your money and effort

590
00:20:05,293 --> 00:20:07,490
into one business or one strategy.

591
00:20:07,670 --> 00:20:10,234
But concentration is also how many people

592
00:20:10,234 --> 00:20:11,234
lose everything.

593
00:20:11,584 --> 00:20:14,083
Stocks through index funds are the most

594
00:20:14,083 --> 00:20:15,300
diversified option.

595
00:20:15,480 --> 00:20:18,336
A single rental property is concentrated.

596
00:20:18,516 --> 00:20:21,585
A business is often the most concentrated

597
00:20:21,585 --> 00:20:24,056
of all, with your job and savings

598
00:20:24,056 --> 00:20:25,029
in one place.

599
00:20:25,378 --> 00:20:28,650
A common approach is to concentrate effort,

600
00:20:28,650 --> 00:20:30,324
but diversify savings.

601
00:20:30,504 --> 00:20:32,899
Work hard on your career or business,

602
00:20:32,899 --> 00:20:35,746
and invest your savings in diversified funds

603
00:20:35,746 --> 00:20:37,946
so one setback can't wipe you out.

604
00:20:38,296 --> 00:20:42,177
Diversification across all three paths is possible

605
00:20:42,177 --> 00:20:42,488
too.

606
00:20:42,668 --> 00:20:44,958
Someone could own index funds,

607
00:20:44,958 --> 00:20:47,553
a rental property and a small side

608
00:20:47,553 --> 00:20:48,240
business.

609
00:20:48,420 --> 00:20:49,470
If one struggles,

610
00:20:49,470 --> 00:20:51,323
the others can carry the load.

611
00:20:51,503 --> 00:20:54,603
Many wealthy families deliberately spread their wealth

612
00:20:54,603 --> 00:20:55,694
this way over time.

613
00:20:56,044 --> 00:20:56,811
Try this.

614
00:20:56,991 --> 00:20:58,372
Look at your total net worth.

615
00:20:58,552 --> 00:21:00,538
If more than half depends on one

616
00:21:00,538 --> 00:21:03,020
asset, consider whether you'd be okay if

617
00:21:03,020 --> 00:21:04,137
that asset failed.

618
00:21:04,486 --> 00:21:06,054
Lesson number fourteen.

619
00:21:06,234 --> 00:21:08,416
Family stories shape your choices.

620
00:21:08,766 --> 00:21:10,963
If your parents lost money in the

621
00:21:10,963 --> 00:21:13,492
stock market, you may distrust stocks.

622
00:21:13,672 --> 00:21:16,282
If they built wealth with rental properties,

623
00:21:16,282 --> 00:21:18,002
real estate may feel natural.

624
00:21:18,182 --> 00:21:19,718
If they ran a business,

625
00:21:19,718 --> 00:21:21,387
you may see it as normal,

626
00:21:21,387 --> 00:21:22,722
or as too stressful.

627
00:21:23,071 --> 00:21:25,176
These stories carry real lessons,

628
00:21:25,176 --> 00:21:27,664
but they're based on specific times and

629
00:21:27,664 --> 00:21:28,110
places.

630
00:21:28,290 --> 00:21:30,517
A crash your parents lived through doesn't

631
00:21:30,517 --> 00:21:31,578
predict your future,

632
00:21:31,578 --> 00:21:33,805
and their success doesn't guarantee yours.

633
00:21:34,154 --> 00:21:36,958
Morgan Housel, in The Psychology of Money,

634
00:21:36,958 --> 00:21:40,363
argues that people's investing decisions are shaped

635
00:21:40,363 --> 00:21:42,900
heavily by their personal experiences,

636
00:21:42,900 --> 00:21:44,569
especially early in life.

637
00:21:44,749 --> 00:21:47,580
Recognizing that helps you make choices based

638
00:21:47,580 --> 00:21:49,404
on evidence, not just memory.

639
00:21:49,754 --> 00:21:51,589
Culture plays a role too.

640
00:21:51,769 --> 00:21:54,190
In some families and communities,

641
00:21:54,190 --> 00:21:56,098
owning land or a home is a

642
00:21:56,098 --> 00:21:57,051
top priority.

643
00:21:57,231 --> 00:21:59,452
In others, owning a business is a

644
00:21:59,452 --> 00:22:00,529
source of pride.

645
00:22:00,709 --> 00:22:02,346
These values are meaningful.

646
00:22:02,526 --> 00:22:04,983
Just make sure the financial side makes

647
00:22:04,983 --> 00:22:07,692
sense, so tradition and math work together.

648
00:22:08,042 --> 00:22:08,983
Try this.

649
00:22:09,163 --> 00:22:11,698
Write down what you believe about stocks,

650
00:22:11,698 --> 00:22:13,244
real estate and business,

651
00:22:13,244 --> 00:22:15,223
and where each belief came from.

652
00:22:15,403 --> 00:22:16,994
Some may be worth keeping.

653
00:22:17,174 --> 00:22:19,020
Some may be worth questioning.

654
00:22:19,369 --> 00:22:20,414
That's angle two.

655
00:22:20,594 --> 00:22:23,572
Tangible comfort, hidden volatility,

656
00:22:23,572 --> 00:22:25,970
survivorship bias, enjoyment,

657
00:22:25,970 --> 00:22:27,211
the sleep test,

658
00:22:27,211 --> 00:22:29,940
concentration and family stories.

659
00:22:30,120 --> 00:22:31,444
Now angle three.

660
00:22:31,624 --> 00:22:32,413
The playbook.

661
00:22:32,763 --> 00:22:33,959
Here's the good news.

662
00:22:34,139 --> 00:22:35,660
You don't have to pick just one.

663
00:22:35,840 --> 00:22:38,230
Many wealthy people use all three at

664
00:22:38,230 --> 00:22:39,358
different stages.

665
00:22:39,538 --> 00:22:41,403
Here are six moves to choose and

666
00:22:41,403 --> 00:22:42,568
combine them wisely.

667
00:22:42,918 --> 00:22:44,439
Move number fifteen.

668
00:22:44,619 --> 00:22:46,477
Start with a stock foundation.

669
00:22:46,826 --> 00:22:47,784
For most people,

670
00:22:47,784 --> 00:22:50,776
stocks through retirement accounts and index funds

671
00:22:50,776 --> 00:22:52,631
are the easiest place to start.

672
00:22:52,811 --> 00:22:55,034
You can begin with small amounts,

673
00:22:55,034 --> 00:22:57,794
get tax advantages and an employer match,

674
00:22:57,794 --> 00:23:00,219
and build wealth without extra work.

675
00:23:00,568 --> 00:23:02,916
This base also protects you if you

676
00:23:02,916 --> 00:23:04,574
later try riskier paths.

677
00:23:04,754 --> 00:23:07,398
If a rental property or business struggles,

678
00:23:07,398 --> 00:23:08,936
you still have a growing,

679
00:23:08,936 --> 00:23:10,965
diversified portfolio behind you.

680
00:23:11,315 --> 00:23:14,737
Many financial planners suggest getting your emergency

681
00:23:14,737 --> 00:23:17,462
fund and retirement savings on track before

682
00:23:17,462 --> 00:23:19,933
taking on large real estate or business

683
00:23:19,933 --> 00:23:20,313
risks.

684
00:23:20,663 --> 00:23:23,218
Stocks also give you a benchmark.

685
00:23:23,398 --> 00:23:25,759
Once you're invested in a low cost

686
00:23:25,759 --> 00:23:28,677
index fund, every other opportunity has to

687
00:23:28,677 --> 00:23:30,135
beat it, after costs,

688
00:23:30,135 --> 00:23:31,525
taxes and your time.

689
00:23:31,705 --> 00:23:34,034
If a rental or business can't clearly

690
00:23:34,034 --> 00:23:36,363
beat simply adding more to your index

691
00:23:36,363 --> 00:23:38,189
fund, it may not be worth the

692
00:23:38,189 --> 00:23:39,448
extra risk and work.

693
00:23:39,798 --> 00:23:40,658
Try this.

694
00:23:40,838 --> 00:23:43,201
Make sure you're getting your full employer

695
00:23:43,201 --> 00:23:45,454
match and investing regularly in low cost

696
00:23:45,454 --> 00:23:47,707
index funds before putting big money into

697
00:23:47,707 --> 00:23:48,477
anything else.

698
00:23:48,826 --> 00:23:50,243
Move number sixteen.

699
00:23:50,423 --> 00:23:52,629
Treat your home as a home first.

700
00:23:52,978 --> 00:23:55,583
Many people consider their house their biggest

701
00:23:55,583 --> 00:23:56,206
investment.

702
00:23:56,386 --> 00:23:59,096
A home can build wealth through mortgage

703
00:23:59,096 --> 00:24:00,789
paydown and appreciation,

704
00:24:00,789 --> 00:24:02,551
and it provides stability.

705
00:24:02,731 --> 00:24:05,306
But it also costs money every year,

706
00:24:05,306 --> 00:24:06,778
in taxes, insurance,

707
00:24:06,778 --> 00:24:08,617
maintenance and interest.

708
00:24:08,967 --> 00:24:10,604
Buying too much house is one of

709
00:24:10,604 --> 00:24:12,662
the most common ways people stall their

710
00:24:12,662 --> 00:24:13,507
wealth building.

711
00:24:13,687 --> 00:24:15,884
A huge mortgage can leave little money

712
00:24:15,884 --> 00:24:16,694
for investing.

713
00:24:17,043 --> 00:24:19,151
Buy a home because you want stability

714
00:24:19,151 --> 00:24:20,518
and it fits your budget,

715
00:24:20,518 --> 00:24:22,398
not just because you expect it to

716
00:24:22,398 --> 00:24:23,766
be your best investment.

717
00:24:24,115 --> 00:24:25,925
Renting isn't throwing money away,

718
00:24:25,925 --> 00:24:26,298
either.

719
00:24:26,478 --> 00:24:28,469
Renting can make sense if you might

720
00:24:28,469 --> 00:24:30,518
move soon, if buying costs much more

721
00:24:30,518 --> 00:24:31,997
than renting in your area,

722
00:24:31,997 --> 00:24:33,875
or if renting lets you invest the

723
00:24:33,875 --> 00:24:34,501
difference.

724
00:24:34,681 --> 00:24:36,614
The best choice depends on prices,

725
00:24:36,614 --> 00:24:38,888
your plans and your discipline to invest

726
00:24:38,888 --> 00:24:39,684
what you save.

727
00:24:40,034 --> 00:24:40,882
Try this.

728
00:24:41,062 --> 00:24:42,382
Before buying a home,

729
00:24:42,382 --> 00:24:44,393
calculate the full monthly cost,

730
00:24:44,393 --> 00:24:47,096
including taxes, insurance and maintenance,

731
00:24:47,096 --> 00:24:49,233
and make sure you can still invest

732
00:24:49,233 --> 00:24:50,176
for retirement.

733
00:24:50,525 --> 00:24:51,977
Move number seventeen.

734
00:24:52,157 --> 00:24:55,071
Try real estate without going all in.

735
00:24:55,420 --> 00:24:57,510
Real estate investment trusts,

736
00:24:57,510 --> 00:25:00,157
or REITs, let you invest in portfolios

737
00:25:00,157 --> 00:25:02,874
of properties through the stock market.

738
00:25:03,054 --> 00:25:05,341
Many REIT index funds are low cost

739
00:25:05,341 --> 00:25:08,570
and diversified, giving you real estate exposure

740
00:25:08,570 --> 00:25:10,252
without being a landlord.

741
00:25:10,602 --> 00:25:12,681
Another option is house hacking.

742
00:25:12,861 --> 00:25:15,085
Buying a small multi unit property,

743
00:25:15,085 --> 00:25:17,245
living in one unit and renting the

744
00:25:17,245 --> 00:25:17,690
others.

745
00:25:17,870 --> 00:25:19,949
The rent can help cover your mortgage,

746
00:25:19,949 --> 00:25:22,027
and you learn the landlord business on

747
00:25:22,027 --> 00:25:22,793
a small scale.

748
00:25:23,142 --> 00:25:24,349
If you buy a rental,

749
00:25:24,349 --> 00:25:25,917
run the numbers carefully.

750
00:25:26,097 --> 00:25:28,318
Include vacancies, repairs,

751
00:25:28,318 --> 00:25:30,950
insurance, taxes and management.

752
00:25:31,130 --> 00:25:34,808
Many new landlords overestimate rent and underestimate

753
00:25:34,808 --> 00:25:35,217
costs.

754
00:25:35,566 --> 00:25:38,109
Learn the landlord rules before you start.

755
00:25:38,289 --> 00:25:41,299
Many states and cities have specific rules

756
00:25:41,299 --> 00:25:42,947
about leases, deposits,

757
00:25:42,947 --> 00:25:44,524
repairs and evictions.

758
00:25:44,704 --> 00:25:47,432
Understanding them protects both you and your

759
00:25:47,432 --> 00:25:50,160
tenants, and helps you avoid costly mistakes.

760
00:25:50,510 --> 00:25:51,335
Try this.

761
00:25:51,515 --> 00:25:53,149
Before buying any rental,

762
00:25:53,149 --> 00:25:55,634
track the local rents and expenses for

763
00:25:55,634 --> 00:25:56,484
three months.

764
00:25:56,664 --> 00:25:59,064
Patience in buying is one of real

765
00:25:59,064 --> 00:26:01,319
estate's most important skills.

766
00:26:01,669 --> 00:26:03,063
Move number eighteen.

767
00:26:03,243 --> 00:26:04,880
Start a business small.

768
00:26:05,229 --> 00:26:09,003
Many successful businesses start as side projects.

769
00:26:09,183 --> 00:26:11,168
Keeping your job while you test an

770
00:26:11,168 --> 00:26:12,979
idea reduces risk dramatically.

771
00:26:13,159 --> 00:26:15,396
You find out whether customers will pay

772
00:26:15,396 --> 00:26:17,002
before risking your savings.

773
00:26:17,352 --> 00:26:20,023
Service businesses, like consulting,

774
00:26:20,023 --> 00:26:22,397
cleaning, landscaping or design,

775
00:26:22,397 --> 00:26:24,771
often need little upfront money.

776
00:26:24,951 --> 00:26:27,048
You can start with skills you already

777
00:26:27,048 --> 00:26:27,331
have.

778
00:26:27,681 --> 00:26:29,681
Set a limit on what you're willing

779
00:26:29,681 --> 00:26:31,269
to lose, in money and time.

780
00:26:31,449 --> 00:26:33,786
If the idea doesn't work within that

781
00:26:33,786 --> 00:26:36,058
limit, stop and try something else.

782
00:26:36,408 --> 00:26:39,586
Buying an existing small business is another

783
00:26:39,586 --> 00:26:40,019
route.

784
00:26:40,199 --> 00:26:42,259
Instead of starting from zero,

785
00:26:42,259 --> 00:26:44,593
you buy a company that already has

786
00:26:44,593 --> 00:26:46,103
customers and profits,

787
00:26:46,103 --> 00:26:47,339
often with a loan.

788
00:26:47,519 --> 00:26:49,009
It has its own risks,

789
00:26:49,009 --> 00:26:51,633
but it skips the most dangerous early

790
00:26:51,633 --> 00:26:52,059
stage.

791
00:26:52,239 --> 00:26:54,931
Some buyers use loan programs backed by

792
00:26:54,931 --> 00:26:57,277
the Small Business Administration.

793
00:26:57,627 --> 00:26:58,417
Try this.

794
00:26:58,597 --> 00:27:00,890
Find one paying customer for your idea

795
00:27:00,890 --> 00:27:03,183
before spending any significant money.

796
00:27:03,363 --> 00:27:05,766
A real customer is the best proof.

797
00:27:06,116 --> 00:27:07,417
Move number nineteen.

798
00:27:07,597 --> 00:27:09,454
Combine them into a flywheel.

799
00:27:09,804 --> 00:27:11,987
Many wealthy people follow a pattern.

800
00:27:12,167 --> 00:27:14,500
They earn income through a career or

801
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:17,221
business, invest the profits in stocks and

802
00:27:17,221 --> 00:27:19,748
real estate, and let those assets grow.

803
00:27:19,928 --> 00:27:21,809
Each path feeds the others.

804
00:27:22,159 --> 00:27:24,542
A business owner might use profits to

805
00:27:24,542 --> 00:27:27,376
buy the building their business operates in,

806
00:27:27,376 --> 00:27:29,180
becoming their own landlord,

807
00:27:29,180 --> 00:27:31,692
while investing the rest in index funds

808
00:27:31,692 --> 00:27:32,980
for diversification.

809
00:27:33,329 --> 00:27:36,225
A worker might max out retirement accounts,

810
00:27:36,225 --> 00:27:37,841
then buy a small rental,

811
00:27:37,841 --> 00:27:40,063
then later start a side business.

812
00:27:40,243 --> 00:27:41,985
Each step builds on the last.

813
00:27:42,334 --> 00:27:44,935
The flywheel works in small steps too.

814
00:27:45,115 --> 00:27:48,115
A side hustle earning five hundred dollars

815
00:27:48,115 --> 00:27:50,472
a month, invested in index funds,

816
00:27:50,472 --> 00:27:53,544
can build a meaningful portfolio over time.

817
00:27:53,724 --> 00:27:56,398
Later, that portfolio could fund a down

818
00:27:56,398 --> 00:27:58,797
payment, or seed a bigger business.

819
00:27:58,977 --> 00:28:00,478
Small wins compound,

820
00:28:00,478 --> 00:28:02,054
just like money does.

821
00:28:02,404 --> 00:28:03,263
Try this.

822
00:28:03,443 --> 00:28:05,034
Sketch your own flywheel.

823
00:28:05,214 --> 00:28:07,101
Where does your money come from today,

824
00:28:07,101 --> 00:28:08,442
and where could it go next?

825
00:28:08,791 --> 00:28:10,068
Move number twenty.

826
00:28:10,248 --> 00:28:12,280
Choose based on your stage of life.

827
00:28:12,630 --> 00:28:14,419
Early on, you may have more time

828
00:28:14,419 --> 00:28:15,034
than money.

829
00:28:15,214 --> 00:28:17,798
Building skills, a career or a small

830
00:28:17,798 --> 00:28:19,951
business can grow your income,

831
00:28:19,951 --> 00:28:22,679
which is the fuel for everything else.

832
00:28:22,859 --> 00:28:26,131
Investing small amounts in index funds keeps

833
00:28:26,131 --> 00:28:27,619
compounding running.

834
00:28:27,968 --> 00:28:29,142
In the middle years,

835
00:28:29,142 --> 00:28:31,019
you may have more money and less

836
00:28:31,019 --> 00:28:31,312
time.

837
00:28:31,492 --> 00:28:34,364
Index funds, retirement accounts and perhaps a

838
00:28:34,364 --> 00:28:36,612
few well chosen properties can work,

839
00:28:36,612 --> 00:28:38,922
without demanding too much attention.

840
00:28:39,272 --> 00:28:40,281
Later in life,

841
00:28:40,281 --> 00:28:43,162
simplicity and income often matter more.

842
00:28:43,342 --> 00:28:46,969
Many people shift toward diversified investments and

843
00:28:46,969 --> 00:28:50,597
steady income, selling or simplifying properties and

844
00:28:50,597 --> 00:28:51,364
businesses.

845
00:28:51,714 --> 00:28:52,882
Whatever your stage,

846
00:28:52,882 --> 00:28:55,393
your income is your biggest wealth building

847
00:28:55,393 --> 00:28:55,685
tool.

848
00:28:55,865 --> 00:28:57,725
Growing it through skills,

849
00:28:57,725 --> 00:29:00,801
promotions or a business creates more money

850
00:29:00,801 --> 00:29:02,517
to invest in every path.

851
00:29:02,697 --> 00:29:04,802
Many people focus too much on returns

852
00:29:04,802 --> 00:29:06,963
and too little on increasing what they

853
00:29:06,963 --> 00:29:08,897
have to invest in the first place.

854
00:29:09,247 --> 00:29:10,048
Try this.

855
00:29:10,228 --> 00:29:11,998
Write down your current stage,

856
00:29:11,998 --> 00:29:13,944
how much time and money you have,

857
00:29:13,944 --> 00:29:15,360
and your risk tolerance.

858
00:29:15,540 --> 00:29:17,756
Then choose the path that fits now,

859
00:29:17,756 --> 00:29:19,592
knowing you can change later.

860
00:29:19,941 --> 00:29:21,433
Imagine three friends,

861
00:29:21,433 --> 00:29:24,076
each with fifty thousand dollars at age

862
00:29:24,076 --> 00:29:24,551
thirty.

863
00:29:24,731 --> 00:29:26,404
The first puts it in a low

864
00:29:26,404 --> 00:29:29,235
cost index fund and keeps investing monthly.

865
00:29:29,415 --> 00:29:31,741
Over twenty years at a seven percent

866
00:29:31,741 --> 00:29:33,163
